// actions.memql
//
// Deployment-namespace AUTHORED ACTIONS -- the world-touching layer of the
// DevOps deployment bundle (epic #2212, I8 #2222). Per the behavioral-
// constructs ADR §2: logic decides, mutations persist, ACTIONS TOUCH THE
// WORLD, automations orchestrate. Each action below is a single external
// capability call rendered from typed args; it calls NO logic/query/mutation/
// automation, touches NO graph, and carries no reactive trigger, so every one
// passes the I7 action-rules validator (component/memql/callgraph) and the
// whole-tree gate (test/dslconformance/callgraph_contract_test.go).
//
// SHAPE (construct-invocation ADR Decision 3, Story 4 / memql#2328). An action
// is reduced to an `args { ... }` block + a SINGLE `capability <verb>(...)`
// call. The bare verb resolves through the file-top
// `use capabilities.<path>.{ verb }` import to a full dotted capability name
// (shell.script, integration.github.tagRelease). The legacy action surfaces are
// gone: `@kind`, `@sideEffect` (the authoritative side-effect class lives on the
// CAPABILITY declaration now -- dsl/capabilities/capabilities.memql, Story 5),
// `intent` (merged into `@description`, the planner's embedding source),
// `params` (renamed `args`), and `argTemplate` / `$params.X` (replaced by the
// typed call args). There is no `body { }` and no `return`.
//
// SURFACE. These actions live under dsl/deployment/, so they inherit the
// `cockpit/runner` policy-default surface with NO per-action annotation (I13,
// component/actions/surfaces.go: deployPackPrefixes). They run OUTSIDE the
// target cluster -- on the operator/CI cockpit runner -- so a deploy never
// executes its own world-touch in-cluster.
//
// CAPABILITY BACKENDS. The shell actions call `capability script(...)` and
// select a NAMED capability script (the I14 contract, #2221) by its `script`
// arg; the action executor's capability-script runner (component/automations/
// steps/capability_script.go) maps that id to an ALLOWLISTED scripts/* path,
// runs it with each rendered arg passed as its own `--name=value` argv element
// and stdin closed (never a JSON-on-stdin blob and never a `sh -c` string), and
// parses the one JSON envelope it writes to stdout. This is
// injection-safe by construction: an author/event value can only pick an
// allowlisted script and travels as data, never as a command line. The
// integration-backed action (tagRelease) resolves to an engine tool instead --
// the same atomic shape.
//
// These actions are CONSUMED by the deploy automation (I10,
// #2224); this file authors the primitives only.

use capabilities.shell.{ script }
use capabilities.integration.github.{ tagRelease }

// =============================================================================
//   Shell-backed deploy actions (capability shell.script)
// =============================================================================

// Backend: scripts/deploy/clone-repo.sh.
/// Check out the MemQL repo at a specific version into a clean workdir on the deploy runner, so the
/// build runs against a pinned ref.
action cloneRepoAtVersion {
  args {
    workdir string!
    ref     string!
    dryRun  boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.cloneRepo", workdir: args.workdir, ref: args.ref, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// buildEngineImage -- build one engine node image for a version. Backend:
// scripts/deploy/build-image.sh (a thin, dry-run-capable wrapper; the real
// release build runs on the GitHub build server, not here -- this is the
// local/no-op surface).
/// Build the container image for one engine node type at a version on the deploy runner
/// (local/dry-run surface) so it can be imported or pushed.
action buildEngineImage {
  args {
    nodeType string!
    version  string!
    workdir  string!
    dryRun  boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.buildImage", nodeType: args.nodeType, version: args.version, workdir: args.workdir, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// importImageToK3d -- load a built node image into the local k3d cluster (the
// docker-local placement step). Backend: scripts/k3d/import-image.sh.
/// Import a built engine node image into the local k3d cluster (development placement) so the
/// development overlay can run it.
action importImageToK3d {
  args {
    nodeType string!
    cluster  string!
    version  string
    dryRun  boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "k3d.importImage", nodeType: args.nodeType, cluster: args.cluster, version: args.version, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// pinOverlayDigests -- pin a kustomize overlay to the exact image digests for a
// release (the GitOps placement step for a deploy target -- local k3d or the
// one cloud overlay, per the `provider` field; there is no staging/production
// pair, epic memql#3943). Backend: scripts/deploy/pin-overlay-digests.sh.
/// Pin a kustomize overlay to the resolved per-node image digests for a release (GitOps placement)
/// so ArgoCD deploys exactly those images.
action pinOverlayDigests {
  args {
    overlayPath string!
    digests     object!
    dryRun  boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "overlay.pinDigests", overlayPath: args.overlayPath, digests: args.digests, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// argoSync -- trigger an ArgoCD application sync to the pinned revision.
// Backend: scripts/deploy/argo-sync.sh.
/// Ask ArgoCD to reconcile an application to a pinned git revision.
action argoSync {
  args {
    app      string!
    revision string!
    dryRun  boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "argocd.sync", app: args.app, revision: args.revision, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// runDeployGate -- run the post-deploy gate and return a structured result
// carrying `passed`. The deploy automation (I10) branches success/rollback on
// logic deployGateGreen, which reads this action's `passed`. Backend:
// scripts/deploy/deploy-gate.sh.
/// Execute the post-deploy verification gate and return a structured pass/fail result indicating
/// whether the deployment is healthy. The provider selects the litmus -- docker-local runs the k3d
/// mesh check, azure runs the in-cluster gate.
action runDeployGate {
  args {
    provider string!
    workdir  string!
    dryRun   boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.gate", provider: args.provider, workdir: args.workdir, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// revertOverlay -- roll an overlay back to a previous deployment's pinned
// digests (owner-only via the deploy automation's spec gate). Backend:
// scripts/deploy/revert-overlay.sh.
/// Restore the overlay's image digests to those of a prior deployment so ArgoCD rolls the cluster
/// back (rollback).
action revertOverlay {
  args {
    overlayPath    string!
    toDeploymentId string!
    dryRun  boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "overlay.revert", overlayPath: args.overlayPath, toDeploymentId: args.toDeploymentId, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// notifyDeploy -- post a deploy status notification. Backend:
// scripts/deploy/notify.sh (a no-op-locally notifier; an integration.slack.*
// backend can replace it where a Slack engine tool is configured).
/// Announce the outcome of a deployment (succeeded/failed) so operators see the result without
/// reading logs.
action notifyDeploy {
  args {
    status       string!
    deploymentId string!
    dryRun  boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.notify", status: args.status, deploymentId: args.deploymentId, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// =============================================================================
//   Substrate actions (capability shell.script) -- memql#4463
// =============================================================================
//
// The actions above place WORKLOADS onto a cluster. The two below act on the
// cluster ITSELF: the Azure substrate it runs on, and how big that substrate
// is. They exist because the first cloud instance was created by hand, so
// nothing in this tree could recreate it -- and when its ArgoCD Application
// pinned a git revision that later vanished, the running cluster became the
// only surviving record of the installation (memql#4463).
//
// They are ordinary deploy-pack actions in every other respect: a single
// capability call over typed args, no graph touch, no logic call, and the
// cockpit/runner surface. That last point is not incidental HERE -- a cluster
// cannot provision itself, and an instance must not drive the roll that
// replaces its own pods, so the runner being OUTSIDE the target cluster is
// what makes these safe to author at all.

// Backend: scripts/deploy/azure-provision.sh.
/// Create or converge the Azure substrate one MemQL instance runs on -- resource group, container
/// registry, key vault, backup storage, AKS cluster, and the workload identities its pods
/// authenticate as. Idempotent: converged infrastructure is left untouched.
action provisionAzureInfrastructure {
  args {
    subscriptionId        string!
    resourceGroup         string!
    clusterName           string!
    registryName          string!
    keyVaultName          string!
    registryResourceGroup string
    backupStorageAccount  string
    location              string
    meshNodeCount         string
    dbNodeCount           string
    dryRun                boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.azureProvision", subscriptionId: args.subscriptionId, resourceGroup: args.resourceGroup, clusterName: args.clusterName, registryName: args.registryName, keyVaultName: args.keyVaultName, registryResourceGroup: args.registryResourceGroup, backupStorageAccount: args.backupStorageAccount, location: args.location, meshNodeCount: args.meshNodeCount, dbNodeCount: args.dbNodeCount, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// Backend: scripts/deploy/azure-scale.sh.
/// Resize an instance: the node count of an AKS node pool, the replica count of the mesh
/// Deployments, or both. Nodes are the cost axis; replicas are the availability axis, and below two
/// replicas a PodDisruptionBudget permits no disruption at all, so node maintenance cannot proceed.
action scaleInstance {
  args {
    subscriptionId string!
    resourceGroup  string!
    clusterName    string!
    nodePool       string
    nodeCount      string
    replicas       string
    namespace      string
    dryRun         boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.azureScale", subscriptionId: args.subscriptionId, resourceGroup: args.resourceGroup, clusterName: args.clusterName, nodePool: args.nodePool, nodeCount: args.nodeCount, replicas: args.replicas, namespace: args.namespace, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// Backend: scripts/deploy/azure-teardown.sh.
/// Delete the Azure substrate of an instance. Destructive, so the confirmation is a PARAMETER that
/// must equal the resource group being deleted -- an automation cannot stop and ask, and a caller
/// that cannot name the target has not decided to delete it. Credential and backup stores are
/// preserved unless deleteStores says otherwise.
action deprovisionAzureInfrastructure {
  args {
    subscriptionId       string!
    resourceGroup        string!
    confirm              string!
    clusterName          string
    keyVaultName         string
    backupStorageAccount string
    deleteStores         boolean
    purgeKeyVault        boolean
    dryRun               boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.azureTeardown", subscriptionId: args.subscriptionId, resourceGroup: args.resourceGroup, confirm: args.confirm, clusterName: args.clusterName, keyVaultName: args.keyVaultName, backupStorageAccount: args.backupStorageAccount, deleteStores: args.deleteStores, purgeKeyVault: args.purgeKeyVault, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// =============================================================================
//   Integration-backed deploy action (capability integration.*)
// =============================================================================

// tagRelease -- cut a git tag + GitHub release for a shipped version. A SIDE-
// EFFECTING integration (its capability class=write, ADR §3 + §7) is reachable
// ONLY through an action; it resolves to the engine's integration tool, not a
// shell script. integration.github.* is control-plane by capability, so it also
// pins to the cockpit/runner surface (I13 deployControlCapabilityPrefixes).
/// Cut a git tag + GitHub release for the version that just deployed.
action tagRelease {
  args {
    repo    string!
    version string!
  }
  capability tagRelease(repo: args.repo, tag: args.version)
}

// =============================================================================
//   Install-phase actions (capability shell.script) -- epic memql#4490
// =============================================================================
//
// `provisionAzureInfrastructure` above builds the substrate. `argoSync` further
// up reconciles an Application. EVERYTHING BETWEEN THOSE TWO POINTS was
// unmodelled, and a real first bring-up found eleven ordered steps living in
// that gap -- six of them dependencies that exist on no manifest in either
// repository (memql#4472, memql#4473).
//
// The actions below own those eleven, and they exist as SEPARATE actions rather
// than one because they fail for different reasons and are re-run for different
// reasons: an operator repairing an instance runs the verifier, an operator
// bumping a version runs the render diff, and neither should have to run
// secret generation to get there.
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS CLASS WORTH SCRIPTING AT ALL is that every one of the six
// undeclared dependencies FAILS SILENTLY. A missing Secret named by a volume
// does not error -- the pod sits in ContainerCreating forever with no log line,
// because the container never starts. An Ingress naming an absent IngressClass
// is a VALID object nothing acts on. A missing ClusterIssuer means the site
// LOADS, with a browser warning. None of them name themselves, and a runbook
// step that a person performs by hand is a step that is skipped exactly once.

// Backend: scripts/deploy/install-cluster-operators.sh.
/// Install the cluster operators and cluster-scoped objects every committed manifest assumes are
/// already present -- cert-manager, CloudNativePG, the External Secrets CRDs and controller,
/// ingress-nginx, the prometheus-operator with this platform's alert rules, and the ACME
/// ClusterIssuer -- from the versions pinned in the repository. Idempotent and verify-first: a
/// converged cluster makes no call that changes anything, and a cluster already running a
/// Prometheus stack keeps its own operator and receives only the rules.
action installClusterOperators {
  args {
    subscriptionId string!
    resourceGroup  string!
    clusterName    string!
    repoRoot       string
    acmeEmail      string
    ingressClass   string
    skipMonitoring boolean
    timeoutSeconds string
    dryRun         boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.installClusterOperators", subscriptionId: args.subscriptionId, resourceGroup: args.resourceGroup, clusterName: args.clusterName, repoRoot: args.repoRoot, acmeEmail: args.acmeEmail, ingressClass: args.ingressClass, skipMonitoring: args.skipMonitoring, timeoutSeconds: args.timeoutSeconds, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// Backend: scripts/deploy/seed-instance-secrets.sh.
/// GENERATE a fresh instance's credentials into its own Key Vault, and create the empty
/// memql-secrets shell the ExternalSecrets merge into. Generation, never migration: a retired
/// vault's entries are another cluster's master key. Create-if-absent, so a redelivered event
/// cannot rotate a live cluster's credentials out from under it; and the DSN and the CNPG
/// bootstrap credential carry ONE password, because two would come up healthy and unable to log in.
action seedInstanceSecrets {
  args {
    keyVaultName   string!
    subscriptionId string
    namespace      string
    dbHost         string
    dbName         string
    dbUser         string
    dbSslMode      string
    skipCluster    boolean
    dryRun         boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.seedInstanceSecrets", keyVaultName: args.keyVaultName, subscriptionId: args.subscriptionId, namespace: args.namespace, dbHost: args.dbHost, dbName: args.dbName, dbUser: args.dbUser, dbSslMode: args.dbSslMode, skipCluster: args.skipCluster, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// Backend: scripts/deploy/wire-external-secrets.sh.
/// Point this instance's External Secrets at THIS instance's vault, authenticating as THIS
/// instance's managed identity -- rendered from provisioning's own esoClientId rather than applied
/// from a committed file carrying another install's literals.
action wireExternalSecrets {
  args {
    keyVaultName   string!
    tenantId       string!
    esoClientId    string!
    namespace      string
    serviceAccount string
    repoRoot       string
    dryRun         boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.wireExternalSecrets", keyVaultName: args.keyVaultName, tenantId: args.tenantId, esoClientId: args.esoClientId, namespace: args.namespace, serviceAccount: args.serviceAccount, repoRoot: args.repoRoot, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// Backend: scripts/deploy/register-gitops-repo.sh.
/// Register an instance's git repository with ArgoCD: the credential Secret whose url must match
/// the Application's repoURL byte for byte, and the AppProject sourceRepos entry without which the
/// Application is refused as "repo not permitted in project" -- a refusal that stops reconciliation
/// while looking harmless. The credential transport is an INPUT, because deploy keys being disabled
/// org-wide is a policy fact a lifecycle step cannot hardcode its way around.
action registerGitOpsRepo {
  args {
    repoUrl       string!
    transport     string!
    project       string
    argoNamespace string
    username      string
    tokenFile     string
    sshKeyFile    string
    secretName    string
    dryRun        boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.registerGitOpsRepo", repoUrl: args.repoUrl, transport: args.transport, project: args.project, argoNamespace: args.argoNamespace, username: args.username, tokenFile: args.tokenFile, sshKeyFile: args.sshKeyFile, secretName: args.secretName, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// Backend: scripts/deploy/verify-install-dependencies.sh.
/// Check BY EXISTENCE that an instance's undeclared install dependencies are present, and report
/// which are not. Existence rather than health is the whole point: the objects were never created,
/// so nothing is unhealthy and ArgoCD has no opinion about a CRD nobody installed. Returns a
/// structured result carrying `passed` and the list of what is missing.
action verifyInstallDependencies {
  args {
    namespace         string
    ingressClass      string
    clusterIssuer     string
    requireIssuer     boolean
    requireMonitoring boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.verifyInstallDependencies", namespace: args.namespace, ingressClass: args.ingressClass, clusterIssuer: args.clusterIssuer, requireIssuer: args.requireIssuer, requireMonitoring: args.requireMonitoring)
}

// Backend: scripts/deploy/settle-after-sync.sh.
/// Wait for the database, restart the mesh to clear accumulated CrashLoopBackOff, and only THEN
/// report sync health. The first sync legitimately fails while the database initialises, so a step
/// that reads the first Degraded as failure is wrong on almost every successful bring-up -- and
/// retrying the sync does not help, because what is needed is time plus a restart.
action settleAfterSync {
  args {
    namespace              string
    dbCluster              string
    argoNamespace          string
    app                    string
    deployments            string
    dbTimeoutSeconds       string
    rolloutTimeoutSeconds  string
    dryRun                 boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.settleAfterSync", namespace: args.namespace, dbCluster: args.dbCluster, argoNamespace: args.argoNamespace, app: args.app, deployments: args.deployments, dbTimeoutSeconds: args.dbTimeoutSeconds, rolloutTimeoutSeconds: args.rolloutTimeoutSeconds, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// Backend: scripts/deploy/render-diff.sh.
/// Render an instance overlay at two engine refs and report the object-set difference BEFORE
/// applying either. A version bump is a migration: one `?ref=` line has cost three retired
/// workarounds, a new object needing a host patch, and a new certificate regime needing real
/// substrate -- none of it discoverable from release notes, all of it discoverable by rendering.
/// Flags NEW objects as well as broken patches, because a new object nobody patches renders with
/// the engine's placeholder and builds cleanly, which is the silent half.
action renderDiff {
  args {
    overlayPath string!
    toRef       string!
    fromRef     string
    acceptKinds string
    outputDir   string
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.renderDiff", overlayPath: args.overlayPath, toRef: args.toRef, fromRef: args.fromRef, acceptKinds: args.acceptKinds, outputDir: args.outputDir)
}

// =============================================================================
//   Release + version provenance (epic memql#4493)
// =============================================================================
//
// A cluster should be able to say what it is running, and a version that was
// cut should have images. Neither held: no node logged a version at boot, and
// thirteen tags between v0.16.0 and v0.19.7 carry no GitHub release -- which
// means no images, because a pushed git tag triggers nothing.

// Backend: scripts/deploy/report-instance-version.sh.
/// Report an instance's DECLARED ref (product.env ENGINE_REF), RENDERED ref (what the overlay
/// composes) and RUNNING images together, plus what the nodes report about themselves. The three
/// are ALLOWED to differ -- a tag's image pins are written before that tag's own images exist, so
/// an instance declaring v0.19.6 legitimately runs 0.19.5 binaries. Quoting any one of them alone
/// answers "what version is running?" with a statement about a different layer, which during an
/// incident is the difference between reading the right diff and the wrong one. Reports rather
/// than refuses: divergence here is usually correct.
action reportInstanceVersion {
  args {
    namespace   string
    overlayPath string
    productEnv  string
    declaredRef string
    dryRun      boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.reportInstanceVersion", namespace: args.namespace, overlayPath: args.overlayPath, productEnv: args.productEnv, declaredRef: args.declaredRef, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}

// Backend: scripts/release/release-engine.sh.
/// Publish a GitHub RELEASE for an engine version and then verify the image build it triggers
/// actually started. A pushed git tag builds NOTHING: build-engine-images is dispatch-only and its
/// single automatic trigger is a `release: [published]` event. The verification is the point -- the
/// bridge is an event handler, and one that silently does not fire is indistinguishable from one
/// that has not fired yet, until the version is deployed and every pod lands in ImagePullBackOff.
/// Idempotent on an already-published release; REFUSES a draft, which emits no publish event.
action releaseEngine {
  args {
    version     string!
    repo        string
    notes       string
    targetSha   string
    pollSeconds string
    dryRun      boolean
  }
  capability script(script: "release.engine", version: args.version, repo: args.repo, notes: args.notes, targetSha: args.targetSha, pollSeconds: args.pollSeconds, dryRun: args.dryRun)
}
